## 6. Machine Learning

Three models were trained to predict game popularity (`log(total_reviews + 1)`) from features: `price`, `is_free`, `genre_encoded`, `avg_playtime_minutes`, `rating`.

**Train/Test split:** 66,428 train | 16,607 test

### Model Results

| Model | RMSE | MAE | R² |
|---|---|---|---|
| Linear Regression | 2.0425 | 1.6164 | 0.1276 |
| Random Forest | 1.6292 | 1.2985 | **0.4449** |

**Random Forest** significantly outperformed Linear Regression, achieving R²=0.4449 compared to 0.1276.

### Feature Importance (Random Forest)

| Feature | Importance |
|---|---|
| avg_playtime_minutes | **0.8624** |
| price | 0.0988 |
| genre_encoded | 0.0374 |
| is_free | 0.0013 |
| rating | 0.0000 |

Average playtime dominates feature importance with 86.2%, suggesting that **how long players engage with a game is the strongest predictor of its popularity**.

### K-Means Clustering (k=4)

| Cluster | Avg Price | Avg log(Reviews) | % Free |
|---|---|---|---|
| 0 | 0.12 | 3.16 | 99% |
| 1 | 53.01 | 4.08 | 0% |
| 2 | 30.46 | 4.17 | 0% |
| 3 | 79.20 | 4.69 | 0% |

- **Cluster 0:** Nearly all free games, lower popularity
- **Clusters 1–3:** Paid games at increasing price points with increasing popularity
- **Cluster 3:** Premium-priced games ($79 avg) with highest average popularity

